Statement: "After this PR, the new key cannot be seen by people who open the lesson page."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. Next.js replaces process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_* with the literal value when it builds the client bundle, so the new key is written into a JavaScript file that every visitor downloads; a search for "llm-live" in the DevTools Sources panel finds it. Even without the prefix, a client component cannot keep a secret: whatever the browser sends in an Authorization header, the person using the browser can read in the Network panel. The fix is to move the call itself: the browser sends POST /challenges/c_17/hints to api.codedev.test with its session, and only the server knows the key. The misconception is that an environment variable is a secret wherever it is read. It stays secret only in code that runs on the server.', 1, 5
