    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nae_121'), 'PR #1287 adds a new prompt file and changes one line of config. prompts/hint-v3.md is not touched (released 2026-09-02).

 prompts/hint-v4.md      | 31 +++++++  (new file)
 config/prompts.ts       |  2 +-
-  hint: { active: "hint-v3@3" },
+  hint: { active: "hint-v4@4" },

Every stored hint has the ref of the template that produced it:

SELECT prompt_ref, count(*) AS answers,
       round(100.0 * avg((rating = ''down'')::int), 1) AS thumbs_down_pct
FROM hint_responses WHERE created_at > now() - interval ''2 days''
GROUP BY prompt_ref;

 prompt_ref | answers | thumbs_down_pct
 hint-v3@3  |   1912  |   4.1
 hint-v4@4  |   2034  |  11.8

Statement: "To go back to the old prompt, setting active back to hint-v3@3 is enough, and the same query will then show whether the thumbs-down rate returns to its earlier level."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Version 3 was released and never edited, so its text is still in the repository exactly as it ran. Rolling back is a one-line config change, not a hunt through git history for the old wording. Because every stored hint carries prompt_ref, the query separates the answers of each version, and after the rollback new rows arrive as hint-v3@3 again. When you compare, look at similar traffic (same days, same challenges), because the mix of learners and challenges changes the rate too. The misconception is that a prompt change can be edited in place and judged by the date of the deploy. With in-place edits, two different texts share one name, and nothing in the data tells them apart.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nae_121') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
