    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nae_221'), 'callModel() in the hint service (sleep waits on the real clock, isTransient() is true for timeouts, 429, 502, 503 and 504):
const TIMEOUT_MS = 10_000;

async function callModel(messages: Message[]): Promise<string> {
  for (let attempt = 1; attempt <= 3; attempt++) {
    try {
      return await llm.complete(messages, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(TIMEOUT_MS) });
    } catch (err) {
      if (attempt === 3 || !isTransient(err)) throw err;
      await sleep(1000 * 2 ** (attempt - 1));
    }
  }
  throw new Error("unreachable");
}

The hint route catches the final error and returns the fallback hint.

Statement: "Because every call has a 10 s timeout, a learner who clicks Hint gets either a hint or the fallback within about 10 s."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. The timeout limits each attempt, not the request. If the provider hangs, attempt 1 waits 10 s, then 1 s of backoff, attempt 2 another 10 s, 2 s of backoff, attempt 3 another 10 s: about 33 s before the fallback. Long before that the learner has clicked again or left, and the lesson page may have given up on its own. A per-call timeout needs an overall deadline for the whole request: each attempt gets min(per-call timeout, time left), and no retry starts when the deadline would pass during it. The misconception is to read a per-call limit as a limit on what the user waits.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nae_221') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
