09:12:04.000 hint req=h_31c0 start deadline=8000ms
09:12:05.200 hint req=h_31c0 call=1 status=429 retry-after=20s error=rate_limit_exceeded (requests per minute)

The retry code:
const waitMs = retryAfterMs ?? backoffMs(attempt);
if (clock.now() + waitMs > deadlineAt) return fallback("rate limited");
await clock.sleep(waitMs);

Statement: "With 6.8 s left and a Retry-After of 20 s, returning the fallback now is the right decision; waiting 20 s and retrying would only make the learner wait past the deadline."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The provider said it will not accept another request from us for 20 s; a retry before then is likely to get another 429 and adds to the rate-limit pressure, and a retry after then lands 12 s past the deadline the hint feature promised. The code compares the wait with the time left and degrades at once: the learner gets the fallback (on CodeDev, the static hint of the current level) and can ask again later. The misconception is that honouring Retry-After means always waiting for it; it means not calling before it, and if that does not fit the deadline, not calling at all.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nae_221') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
