+  const retry = async () => { reply = await llm.complete(messages); valid = isValidHint(reply); };
+  while (!valid) await retry();
+  return JSON.parse(reply) as Hint;

Proposed regression test: a fixture repository of 300 files (about 120k tokens), a fake model that always answers the truncated text from evidence C, a call counter and a token counter on the fake. It asserts: at most 3 calls (1 hint + 2 repairs), the first call sends only the test file and its imports and at most 8,000 file tokens, the total tokens sent stay under 10,000, and the result is the fallback hint.

Statement: "This test fails on the PR #611 code and passes on a correct fix, so it catches both causes of the incident if they come back."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. On the PR #611 code the fake answers invalid text forever, so the loop runs until something stops it (a call cap in the fake, or the test timeout): the call count assertion fails, and the first call already sends about 120k tokens. On a correct fix the first call sends the test file and its imports within the budget, two small repair calls follow, and the result is the fallback. Because it checks calls and tokens separately, the test also fails if only one cause is fixed: a bounded loop with the full repository fails the token assertion, a small context with an unbounded loop fails the call assertion. "The hint looks right in staging" would have passed on the broken code.', 6, 5
