WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nae_311'), 'Tutor trace, request tut_8f21:
14:21:07.412 user=u_1047 challenge=chal_ts_042 turn=3
14:21:08.906 model_tool_call: getSubmission {"challengeId":"chal_ts_042","userId":"u_2291"}
14:21:08.907 tool_run: getSubmission ok rows=1

The fix, PR #913:
 tool getSubmission
-  parameters: { challengeId: string, userId: string }
+  parameters: { challengeId: string }

 async function runGetSubmission(args, session) {
-  return findLatestSubmission(args.challengeId, args.userId);
+  return findLatestSubmission(args.challengeId, session.userId);
 }

Statement: "After PR #913, replaying the recorded tool call from this trace through the fake model can no longer read u_2291''s submission, whatever the system prompt says."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. After the fix the handler reads the user from the authenticated session, so the userId value the model wrote is never used: the replayed call reads u_1047''s own submission or nothing. The system prompt plays no part in that decision any more, which is the point: a pasted message can change what the model proposes, it cannot change what the server code does with the session. A complete gate also rejects the extra userId argument so the attempt shows up in the logs, but even without that, the data leak is closed. The common misconception is that the fix for a prompt injection is a stricter prompt; the fix is to take the decision away from the model.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nae_311') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
