    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nae_421'), 'Gateway log for two requests 40 seconds apart, same prompt prefix (system instructions, examples and the challenge text, 1,800 tokens), different learner code at the end:

req_a  input_tokens=2100  cached_input_tokens=0     output_tokens=164  hint="Check the order of words with the same count."
req_b  input_tokens=2080  cached_input_tokens=1792  output_tokens=171  hint="Your loop skips the last word. What does split give you?"

Statement: "req_b was answered from the provider''s cache: the stored answer of req_a was reused, so req_b cost no output tokens and only the 288 uncached input tokens."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. Provider prompt caching reuses the processing of an identical prompt prefix, here the 1,792 tokens of instructions, examples and challenge text that both requests start with. The model still reads the rest of the input and generates a new answer: req_b has its own hint and 171 billed output tokens. Cached input tokens are usually billed at a lower rate (the discount, the minimum prefix length and how long the cache lives depend on the provider), not removed from the bill. A response cache in your own code is a different thing: it skips the call and returns a stored answer for an identical key, which is only right when the whole input is the same. The misconception is that "cached" in the usage log means the answer was reused.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nae_421') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
