  You are a tutor helping {{learnerName}} with the challenge "{{challengeTitle}}".
  ...
  Learner code:
  {{learnerCode}}

Cache key: hint:v4:model-a-2026-08:t0:<sha256 of normalized learnerCode + failingTest>

Hint log:
09:12  user=u_1042  key=hint:v4:model-a-2026-08:t0:9c1e...  source=model  text="Hi Lan, look at the tie-break in your sort."
09:40  user=u_2210  key=hint:v4:model-a-2026-08:t0:9c1e...  source=cache  text="Hi Lan, look at the tie-break in your sort."

Statement: "u_2210 received a hint addressed to another learner because the prompt puts the learner''s name into the answer while the cache key, which has no user in it, lets two learners with the same code share one entry."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. Both learners submitted the same code for the same failing test, so the normalized input and the key are identical, and u_2210 got the stored answer. That answer was generated for u_1042 from a prompt that contains their name, so the cached value carries personal data from one learner to another. Two fixes are correct: remove the name from the prompt (a hint does not need it, and the cache can then be shared), or keep the name and add the user to the key, which makes every entry private and lowers the hit rate to almost nothing. Hashing the key does not help, because the problem is in the value. The misconception is that a cache is safe when its key contains no personal data; the stored answer must not contain any either.', 3, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_nae_421') AND order_index = 3)
    RETURNING id
)
